				xmlns:pdfaExtension="http://www.aiim.org/pdfa/ns/extension/"
				xmlns:pdfaSchema="http://www.aiim.org/pdfa/ns/schema#"
				xmlns:pdfaProperty="http://www.aiim.org/pdfa/ns/property#">
			<pdf:Producer>pdfTeX</pdf:Producer>
			<pdf:Keywords>AI agents, web automation, bot mitigation, prompt injection, adversarial defense, browser agents</pdf:Keywords>
			<dc:format>application/pdf</dc:format>
			<dc:title>
				<rdf:Alt>
					<rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Mitigating Unwanted Web Traffic from Bots</rdf:li>
				</rdf:Alt>
			</dc:title>
			<dc:creator>
				<rdf:Seq>
					<rdf:li>Emnet Mehari Tekeste</rdf:li>
				</rdf:Seq>
			</dc:creator>
			<dc:language>
				<rdf:Bag>
					<rdf:li>en</rdf:li>
				</rdf:Bag>
			</dc:language>
			<dc:rights>
				<rdf:Alt>
					<rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Â© 2025. This work is licensed under a CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 license.</rdf:li>
				</rdf:Alt>
			</dc:rights>
			<dc:description>
				<rdf:Alt>
					<rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">The growth of a new iteration of AI-powered bots capable of leveraging artificial intelligence for contextual understanding, task planning, and autonomous interaction has rendered obsolete traditional defenses against web robots, such as CAPTCHA and robots.txt. This thesis explores different adversarial defenses that target vulnerabilities in how these bots, or the AIs at their core, process, understand, and interact with the web. We develop and test a defense framework implemented as middleware and consisting of three modules: text perturbation to disrupt parsing by inserting junk characters, semantic injection to disrupt reasoning by inserting misleading prompts, and element protection to disrupt verification by concealing page elements used for task confirmation. The framework was evaluated against three AI-browser tools across three tasks, measuring their ability to perform these tasks on two custom-deployed websites with different defenses in place. Results demonstrate partial effectiveness on text-heavy summarization tasks, but negligible impact on structured data extraction and interactive ones. Vision capabilities consistently bypassed text-based defenses. Critically, the defenses rendered screen readers unusable, creating unacceptable accessibility barriers. We conclude that lightweight, generalizable adversarial defenses are insufficient against current AI capabilities. None of the tested browsers requested robots.txt or identified themselves as automated agents, confirming the inadequacy of compliance-based approaches. Effective countermeasures likely require either computationally expensive techniques or enforceable regulations governing AI web access. </rdf:li>
				</rdf:Alt>
			</dc:description>
			<dc:subject>
				<rdf:Bag>
					<rdf:li>AI agents, web automation, bot mitigation, prompt injection, adversarial defense, browser agents</rdf:li>
				</rdf:Bag>
			</dc:subject>
			<pdfaid:conformance>B</pdfaid:conformance>
			<pdfaid:part>2</pdfaid:part>
			<xmp:CreatorTool>LaTeX with hyperref</xmp:CreatorTool>
			<xmp:CreateDate>2025-12-29T13:02:45+00:00</xmp:CreateDate>
